, and is labeled as the direction of motion or attempted motion. A red arrow pointing toward the left is located near the bottom left corner of the crate, at the interface between that corner and the supporting surface and is labeled as f. A magnified view of a bottom corner of the crate and the supporting surface shows that the roughness in the two surfaces leads to small gaps between them. There is direct contact only at a few points.">
<button type="button" aria-label="Click to enlarge image of The figure shows a crate on a flat surface. A black arrow points toward the right, away from the crate, and is labeled as the direction of motion or attempted motion. A red arrow pointing toward the left is located near the bottom left corner of the crate, at the interface between that corner and the supporting surface and is labeled as f. A magnified view of a bottom corner of the crate and the supporting surface shows that the roughness in the two surfaces leads to small gaps between them. There is direct contact only at a few points." class="image-button-wrapper"><img src="/apps/image-cdn/v1/f=webp/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/a30e490a026c14749890033387747f7d76e09291" data-media-type="image/jpeg" alt="The figure shows a crate on a flat surface. A black arrow points toward the right, away from the crate, and is labeled as the direction of motion or attempted motion. A red arrow pointing toward the left is located near the bottom left corner of the crate, at the interface between that corner and the supporting surface and is labeled as f. A magnified view of a bottom corner of the crate and the supporting surface shows that the roughness in the two surfaces leads to small gaps between them. There is direct contact only at a few points." width="575" height="236" loading="lazy" data-original-src="/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/a30e490a026c14749890033387747f7d76e09291"></button>
</span>

<figcaption class="os-caption-container">
  <span class="os-title-label">Figure </span>
<span class="os-number">6.10</span>



  <span class="os-divider"> </span>
  <span class="os-caption">Frictional forces, such as <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math> always oppose motion or attempted motion between objects in contact. Friction arises in part because of the roughness of the surfaces in contact, as seen in the expanded view. For the object to move, it must rise to where the peaks of the top surface can skip along the bottom surface. Thus, a force is required just to set the object in motion. Some of the peaks will be broken off, also requiring a force to maintain motion. Much of the friction is actually due to attractive forces between molecules making up the two objects, so that even perfectly smooth surfaces are not friction-free. (In fact, perfectly smooth, clean surfaces of similar materials would adhere, forming a bond called a “cold weld.”)</span>
</figcaption></figure></div>
<p id="fs-id1165038192661">The magnitude of the frictional force has two forms: one for static situations (static friction), the other for situations involving motion (kinetic friction). What follows is an approximate empirical (experimentally determined) model only. These equations for static and kinetic friction are not vector equations.</p>
<div data-type="note" id="fs-id1165036855503" class="ui-has-child-title"><header><h3 class="os-title" data-type="title">
  <span class="os-title-label" data-type="">Magnitude of Static Friction</span>
</h3></header><section>
<div class="os-note-body">
<p id="fs-id1165037982016">The magnitude of static friction <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is</p>
<div data-type="equation" id="fs-id1165037176402"><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math><div class="os-equation-number">
  <span class="os-number">6.1</span>
</div>
</div>
<p id="fs-id1165038241878">where <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is the <span data-type="term" class="no-emphasis" id="term-00004" group-by="c">coefficient of static friction</span> and <em data-effect="italics">N</em> is the magnitude of the normal force.</p>
</div></section></div>
<p id="fs-id1165037973917">The symbol <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mo>≤</mo></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> means <em data-effect="italics">less than or equal to</em>, implying that static friction can have a maximum value of <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mi>N</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mi>N</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> Static friction is a responsive force that increases to be equal and opposite to whatever force is exerted, up to its maximum limit. Once the applied force exceeds</p>
<p id="fs-id1165038033332"><span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mtext>(max),</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mtext>(max),</mtext></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> the object moves. Thus,</p>
<div data-type="equation" id="fs-id1165036866864" class="unnumbered" data-label=""><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>max</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mi>N</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>max</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mi>N</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></div>
<div data-type="note" id="fs-id1165038277974" class="ui-has-child-title"><header><h3 class="os-title" data-type="title">
  <span class="os-title-label" data-type="">Magnitude of Kinetic Friction</span>
</h3></header><section>
<div class="os-note-body">
<p id="fs-id1165037149957">The magnitude of kinetic friction <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is given by</p>
<div data-type="equation" id="fs-id1165038157609"><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math><div class="os-equation-number">
  <span class="os-number">6.2</span>
</div>
</div>
<p id="fs-id1165038293964">where <sp